WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.113
Drip, drip, drip.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.000
It's springtime in the Pacific Northwest.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.163
How delightful.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.040
It's often said, by me,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.850
that if you can't make
it in Silicon valley,

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.200
then you head to Seattle.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.763
And if you can't make it
there, you go to Vancouver.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.680
There's a reason housing prizes

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.540
are only mildly insane in these parts

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.723
as opposed to totally
insane in Silicon Valley.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.300
You have to put up with things

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.300
like weather, Jeff
Bezos's big silver spheres

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.725
and tourist spots where people
throw fish at your face.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.960
Still, as a Californian,
I'm willing to concede

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.880
that the Pacific Northwest
might have some merits.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.300
I mean, this is decent.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.410
And this pizza designed by a wizard

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.913
probably tastes pretty good.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.560
And so I have set out on a road trip

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.880
from Seattle to Vancouver

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.423
to find out why people
choose to live here.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.130
Why they have given their souls

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.940
over to Microsoft and Amazon.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.730
And why they're so open

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.170
about caging humans
and machines like this,

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.003
just so they can build our
dead eyed replacements.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.130
Tourists would probably start

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.103
their trip hanging out in Seattle.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.220
But I'm a tech dork with
a time lapse to make.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.570
And so we're going ahead
east to Redmond and Bellevue

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.423
also known as the towns
that Microsoft built.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.630
Microsoft's headquarters in Redmond

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.920
has all the we're doing
technology essentials.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.543
There are slogans, and more slogans.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.560
There's a garage placed
randomly inside of an office

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.053
because startups come from garages.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.163
There are buildings and more buildings.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.810
And there are treehouses
where engineers go

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.450
to meditate on how to next level excel

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.933
and how to let Skype keep languishing.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.283
Nearby Bellevue is similar,
but kind of ritzier.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.480
This for example, is the shopping mall

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.963
in the bad part of town.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.063
I have come to Bellevue to see this thing.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.760
But mostly to meet the
man who paid for it.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.620
How are you Nathan?

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.150
Great, how are you doing?

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.490
Nathan Myhrvold.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:13.483
Yeah, likewise.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:15.470
Nathan is the former

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.913
Chief Technology Officer at Microsoft.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.230
Today, he's perhaps best known

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.623
as the world's most curious human.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.310
This is a microscope that I built.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.610
These are commercial microscopes

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.780
that I then heavily modified.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.970
And this is a solar telescope.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.230
You can see incredible amounts of detail

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.150
of the sun churning.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.310
Now this device here is a Panorama Robot.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.293
I use this for taking landscape pictures.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.990
And this is a four camera
array that we built.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.027
This goes onto this mount here,

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:58.630
and that mount slowly goes
across the sky at night.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:00.173
So you track the stars.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:07.190
So what we see are these
wonderful long meteor that-

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.890
Nathan really does do a bit of everything.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.513
And he does everything to the extreme.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.300
He's an accomplished paleontologist

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.343
and has dug up numerous
dinosaur skeletons.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.877
But just finding the bones is not enough.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.770
So back in 1997, I was interested

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.250
in the following question which is,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.320
there are a bunch of the dinosaurs

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.940
called Sauropod dinosaurs

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.050
which had really long skinny necks.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.120
So Apatosaurus, also called Brontosaurus

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.870
sometimes is an example.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.470
Those had really long skinny tails.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.860
And a British biomechanics expert,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.070
he wondered if they could
have been used to make noise.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.260
So I made a computer
model that showed that

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.360
in fact, the tail of
these Sauropod dinosaurs

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.083
could actually act like
a whip, a bull whip.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.240
Now the way a bull whip makes

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.320
that cracking noise.
Yeah.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.720
Is because the tip of the whip

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:17.920
actually goes about twice

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.320
the speed of sound.
Okay.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.253
And that crack is a sonic boom.

00:05:26.456 --> 00:05:29.060
So I published a paper on this in 1997.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.740
It was well received,
but also controversial.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.730
A lot of dinosaur
paleontologists said, well,

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:39.440
I won't believe it until
you build a physical model.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.570
I said, okay, you wanna physical model?

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:43.629
I'll make a physical model.

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:48.040
So this replicates the
tail of a Apatosaurus.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:54.030
And so the idea is as
this animal would walk,

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.063
this very long very thin tail
could be made whip around.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.373
We have a high speed video camera.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.170
We have shown with that
high speed video camera

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.453
that this went mock too.

00:06:12.076 --> 00:06:13.760
Do they think it's like a defense thing

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.360
or like to make noise to signal?

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.010
Well, okay, so it turns
out half of the specimens

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.610
of this kind of dinosaur
have intervertebral damage

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.210
at about number 23, somewhere down here.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.960
It turns out that same area

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.250
is the area where a bull whip wears out.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.220
So about half the specimens
were whipping their tails.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.053
Oh wow.

00:06:36.053 --> 00:06:38.860
So often that they damaged their bones.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.163
Which totally sounds
like male behavior to me.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.550
I think the way they got a date

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:50.790
was to impress the females
by making loud booms.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.380
And if you do a scaling argument,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:57.110
you can show that the noise
would've been deafening.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:01.863
Like as loud as the big
guns on the USS New Jersey.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.510
So here's the other interesting
thing about wild animals.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.373
You can't know for sure
what you're going to get.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.810
Nathan also likes to photograph wildlife.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.850
I decided I wanted to photograph all

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.460
of the wild cats and
wild dogs in the world.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.290
But all the species, no,

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:33.540
the individuals.
Yeah.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.670
So this is a camera trap.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.450
So this is a camera that you
set up and you leave out there

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:44.713
and it gets automatically
triggered if the animal walks by.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.960
That's really great
for really shy animals.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.973
And unfortunately, most
wild cats are really shy.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:57.820
And this is a rig that
we call the three Amigos.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:01.790
So we have three, 600 millimeter lenses.

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.070
We have wired them together

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.530
with electronics box that we build.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.520
So with this, I can take a picture

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.140
which will simultaneously be
taken by all three cameras.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.430
And that allows me to make a big panorama.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:15.390
I see, sure.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.393
Of a moving subject.
Okay.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.610
So these are Sandhill Cranes.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.863
There's tens of thousands
of them in this one photo.

00:08:25.999 --> 00:08:28.593
You couldn't get a picture
like this without that rig.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.120
And Nathan is into snowflakes as well.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.303
Like, really into snowflakes.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:47.968
Most of the water that
we consume falls as snow.

00:08:47.968 --> 00:08:51.480
Okay, the reason we get any
water at all in the summer

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.483
in the Northwest is cause
the snow pack is melting.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.550
And I'd done a lot of microscopy.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:08:59.480
So, oh this is great.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.020
I'll take some pictures of snowflakes.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.920
Well, it turns out that's really hard.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.950
So after lots of experimentation
and effort, we built this.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.906
So you have to catch the snowflake and-

00:09:17.906 --> 00:09:20.520
And here's how you catch a snowflake.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.520
This is a very delicate sable brush.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.530
And you put this down,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.780
and as it gets close to the snowflake,

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:34.080
static electricity makes
the snowflake jump up

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.542
and it touches that.

00:09:35.542 --> 00:09:36.375
And then it's in here is-

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:37.520
And then we put it on here

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.850
and we have to put it on a slide

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.210
that's made out of artificial sapphire.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.810
Then we have our lighting rig

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:48.210
made with an exotic set
of light emitting diodes.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.080
Which we're able to pulse very, very fast.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.590
Then we have a computer
controlled mechanism

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:57.530
and it takes a series of pictures quickly.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.510
So this is some of the results.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.860
And these are actually the
highest resolution photographs

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:06.350
of snowflakes ever taken on
our first snowflake shoot.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.440
When the first one came out,

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.210
I turned to my two
compatriots who were with me.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.900
I said, look, I don't know how great

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.140
this is gonna be going forward,

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:16.094
but we definitely have our
Christmas card for this year.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:23.640
These days, Nathan spends

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.290
a lot of his time in the kitchen.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.900
He's an accomplished chef who has produced

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.433
a stunning series of cookbooks
called Modernist Cuisine.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:39.180
The books are famous for how big they are.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.693
They're amazing photos.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.203
And the science and
techniques they describe.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:49.663
Nathan's most recent
book centers on pizza.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.570
Well, pizza's probably

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.070
the single most popular dish on earth.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:58.460
The other thing about pizza is that

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.560
there's tons of lore and legend

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.405
and oh, you can't make pizza

00:11:02.405 --> 00:11:04.480
unless you have the water of Naples

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.520
or your oven was constructed

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:09.200
of special stone quarry near Naples.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.960
Those sorts of myths and legends

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:14.010
are one of the things
that we love to dig into

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.530
because sometimes there's
a germ of truth to it.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.410
In which case it's really interesting.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.503
And in other cases, it's just hooey.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.950
So this is our pizza scanner.
Okay.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.370
So it turns out there's
different things you can do

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:34.120
in the crust on different
techniques of baking

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:38.640
that'll make the rim either
puffier or less puffy.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.830
Well, I wanted to measure that.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.670
So it's putting a structured
light field on it.

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:46.333
Very much like a light
arm in a self-driving car.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.050
And this one, this is through stretching.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.450
So this is a called a texture analyzer.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:56.090
What this is does, is
it presses on a subject

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.800
and it measures the force as it presses.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:02.660
And so you get a force
versus displacement curve.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:04.400
And from that curve you can tell off

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:06.980
the crispiness or chewiness

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.180
What else do we have?

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.320
We got a centrifuge.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.800
What do you use this for?

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:16.530
So here is ground up peas.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:20.230
So this is frozen peas
from the supermarket

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:23.070
and we puree them into goo.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:24.380
Well, then when you spin them

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:28.630
in the centrifuge you get this.

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:29.830
And you can like extract that out

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:31.940
and make that this concentrated
sort of essence of-

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:33.503
Yes, and it's fantastic.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.637
And then this is called
a rotary evaporator.

00:12:37.637 --> 00:12:40.860
Rotary evaporators are
basically used for distillation.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:44.920
So we have got some tomatoes
that we have ground up.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.940
It spins in here and we suck a vacuum

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:49.950
and then we carefully cool it.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:52.420
And that allows us to concentrate it.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.610
And here is the concentrate.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:58.063
So I would invite you to taste some.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.833
Should taste like a fresh tomato.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:04.863
Wow, yeah.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:09.163
Nathan's pizza pal is Francisco Migoya.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.700
He's a world class pastry chef

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:15.950
who used to work at French Laundry.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.001
I'm gonna make a couple
pizzas for you to see.

00:13:18.001 --> 00:13:22.290
We're gonna do not classical
toppings on them as well,

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.610
so that you can see more or less-

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:25.443
Science the shit

00:13:25.443 --> 00:13:27.360
out of this pizza.
Okay.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.860
Actually hand us a double.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:33.160
So it's gotta be very supple,
but it's also very light.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:34.570
That's because we've allowed it

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:37.150
to slowly build up the gas in it.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:41.120
We always ferment overnight
in a refrigerator.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.300
It makes a huge, huge difference.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.350
So it really should be very supple.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:47.600
You see how Francisco is working on that.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:48.810
Yeah, yeah.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:51.010
So we're basically trying to stretch this

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.100
without making a tooth in either.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.170
We're trying to leave also a bit of a rim.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.870
Cause this is gonna puff up when it bakes.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.450
This is basically an Alfredo sauce

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:01.095
that you would typically put in pasta.

00:14:01.095 --> 00:14:02.120
It's one of favorites.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.460
And this is smoke paprika mozzarella.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:06.538
Fresh mozzarella.

00:14:06.538 --> 00:14:07.371
Oh, wow, okay.

00:14:07.371 --> 00:14:08.750
Yeah, we made a whole set of

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.270
our own flavored mozzarellas.

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:12.103
All right, so I'm gonna go

00:14:12.103 --> 00:14:13.423
in the oven now.
Okay.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:17.784
How hot is it?

00:14:17.784 --> 00:14:20.117
This is at 840 Fahrenheit.

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:28.040
So there you have it.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.140
Smells amazing.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:32.280
So this is Iberico Ham.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.100
We also did the pea butter.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.410
That's what we put on
top and some pea shoots.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:37.973
Okay, well let's try some.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.120
It's delicious.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:50.120
I like kind of the saltiness
of the ham with the pea.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.370
And that's like the best
crust I think I've ever had.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:54.680
I mean, so light.

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.130
Yeah, and there's so much flavor in there.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:57.963
You've time for another one?

00:14:57.963 --> 00:14:59.313
Yes please.
Yep, okay.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.540
So here we have an ingredient

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.630
called pressure caramelized zucchini.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:14.410
We've pressure cooked it, and
we've added some baking soda.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:19.260
And the reason is the reactions
that cause food to brown.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.240
A chemist would say it
was the Maillard Reaction.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.960
So if you add some baking soda

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:25.810
and pressure cook the zucchini,

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:29.680
the natural sugars and
zucchini will maillardize

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:31.753
and that puts a unique flavor in it.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:38.313
It's really good.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:40.730
I mean, you're not gonna,

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:43.550
you have no motivation,
like open up pizza place or-

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:47.330
So my usual joke is, as a cookbook writer,

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:50.020
I'm familiar with one capital intensive,

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.810
often money losing business.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:52.750
I don't need to be in a second one.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.870
Yeah.
But if you're passionate

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:57.630
and curious about pizza,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.840
you will learn all kinds
of things in the book

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:01.360
you'll find nowhere else

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.660
Were there some things you've discovered

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:07.250
that were kind of myths about pizza?

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:10.220
The water that we used to make a pizza.

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:12.823
We made pizza with water
from a swimming pool.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:15.160
Now, I don't recommend

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.980
that you use swimming
pool water, but it worked.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:20.193
Didn't kill the yeast.

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:30.900
Away from the kitchen,

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.233
Nathan heads up Intellectual Ventures.

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:36.210
It's a very controversial company

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.563
that hires very bright
people to think up ideas.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:44.713
Like people literally sit in a
room and think up inventions.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.533
And then Intellectual
Ventures patents the ideas.

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:52.763
Nathan says he does this
to let ideas flourish.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.230
Other people say he's a patent troll

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:59.450
because he's trying to collect
as many big ideas as possible

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:02.393
so that IV can charge
people for access to them.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.310
So there were a bunch of tech companies,

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.910
particularly large tech companies

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.270
that had the attitude of look,

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:13.220
if we wanna use ideas in our products,

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:14.783
that's our prerogative.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.010
Who the hell are you, inventor?

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:19.953
All you did was come up with the idea.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:24.850
I think that if you create a situation

00:17:24.850 --> 00:17:27.210
where big tech companies can steal

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:30.550
any invention they want
to, that's not right.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:31.540
It's morally not right.

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:34.260
But I think it's also less efficient

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.410
at getting new ideas for the world.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:40.390
Yeah, so you actually were born in Seattle

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:42.810
but grew up in Southern California.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:43.726
Yeah, that's right.

00:17:43.726 --> 00:17:46.100
My joke is that I'm like a salmon.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.450
I had to come back to
the home stream to spawn.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.240
I know by the time you were 14,

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.430
I think you'd already skipped four grades

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.130
and graduated high school

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.000
and you were heading off to college.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:07.000
You pursued a couple fields
of very hardcore science.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:10.510
I have a bachelor's degree, a master's,

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.060
a second master's and a PhD.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:13.660
And they're all in different topics.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:15.770
But the last one was physics.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:20.500
And I was a postdoc with
Steven Hawking at Cambridge.

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:24.920
And then I wound up starting
a software company in 1983.

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:28.750
Then Microsoft acquired our company.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.170
Then went to work for Bill Gates.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:32.960
So you're not a computer
scientist by training.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:34.560
You get to Microsoft, you end up

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.860
as the Chief Technology Officer.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.040
But then you have all these
interests clearly as we can see.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.563
So is that what was
bubbling inside of you.

00:18:44.563 --> 00:18:47.010
Just this need to go off and try.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:48.160
Go explore all these things

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:49.460
that you're fascinated by.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:52.360
I loved my time at Microsoft.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:57.360
But I did have to put 90% of
my waking time into one thing.

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:01.720
And I am naturally interested
in lots of other things.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.420
One of the biggest things you have pursued

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:07.040
at Intellectual Ventures and
through sort of your friendship

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:09.280
and various partnerships with Bill Gates,

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.320
a lot of it's around climate change

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:14.560
and technology related to that.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.800
You've had big ideas around geoengineering

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:18.950
and I've seen you guys talk

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:23.010
about the filling the
atmosphere with particles to-

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:25.913
Well, so here's a way to
think about global warming.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.290
Sunlight leaves the sun.

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:34.040
It comes roaring across the
93 million miles of space.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:35.140
It hits earth.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:39.200
Some of it then is re-radiated
as infrared radiation.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:40.677
And then infrared radiation comes up

00:19:40.677 --> 00:19:43.730
and it hits CO2 in the
atmosphere and it gets stuck.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:45.920
That's what global warming is about.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.100
Well, the amount of energy

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:48.980
the sun is depositing here on earth

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.180
is about 300 watts per square meter.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:55.580
The amount of extra
radiation that is trapped

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:58.140
by this extra CO2 in an atmosphere

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:01.680
is somewhere within two and
three watts per square meter.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:03.560
It's 1%.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:05.980
So it's a little effect, but of course

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:08.260
it compounds across all of earth.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.020
And that keeps building up over time

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:12.090
and that's what eventually screws you.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.930
Well, that means one approach to this

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.800
is we just have to make the sun 1% dimer.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:21.635
Well, how do you control the sun?

00:20:21.635 --> 00:20:23.050
The answer is you could control that

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:25.590
by putting particles
in the high atmosphere.

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:30.590
And human eyes really aren't
sensitive to a 1% change.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.690
You need almost a factor
of two change in light

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:35.800
for us to see a difference.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.850
So if we bounced that off,

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:40.820
you literally couldn't tell here on earth

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:42.520
that you'd done it.

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:44.490
There's another set of
ideas that are about

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.913
how you could capture CO2
directly out of the atmosphere.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:57.586
Many parts of the ocean
are very nutrient poor.

00:20:57.586 --> 00:21:00.236
They're nutrient poor
primarily cause they lack iron.

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:04.020
So you can do something
called iron fertilization.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.390
You put dissolved iron,
similar to what a human

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:08.870
would take for an iron
supplement if you're anemic.

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:11.070
And you put that into the sea water

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:14.610
and immediately enormous
amounts of plankton bloom.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:17.380
Those blooming plankton feed lots of fish.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:19.910
So you help the whole ecosystem that way.

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:22.640
But then quite a bit of those plankton die

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:23.870
and they fall down to
the bottom of the ocean,

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:25.503
carrying their carbon with them.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:28.100
I mean, I think the general idea, probably

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.210
that a lot of people have is

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:33.020
we do something like that
just by nature of being humans

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:36.080
we're gonna **** it up even worse somehow.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.320
Well, maybe it turns out

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:41.720
that geoengineering has
got such bad side effects,

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:44.305
we never decide to use it.

00:21:44.305 --> 00:21:45.593
But we gotta know.

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:53.520
There's a lot of people
in the climate community

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:55.640
that hate mention of geoengineering.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:59.020
Right.
A lot of climate folks

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:03.630
think that if only we
stopped driving SUVs,

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.670
lived simple lives, et cetera.

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:10.313
We could just decide to
not have climate change.

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.140
The trouble is climate
change isn't a decision.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.430
There's some very hard physics.

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:18.720
So you can't just say,
oh, we'll all go green.

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:19.553
And it'll all be great.

00:22:19.553 --> 00:22:22.360
And we don't have to ever
worry about geoengineering

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.260
2050 is a date for various reasons

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.150
that gets focused on a lot.

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:28.780
So it's 2050, are we ****ed

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:29.680
or are we okay?

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.603
What's your, If you're betting.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:34.270
I think by 2050 we will have

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:37.240
very substantial temperature increase.

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:42.120
And we will either have enormous
environmental consequences,

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:44.143
huge loss of human life or both.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.380
And I think we really need to say

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:50.150
what are our alternatives.

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:53.570
Because with the crazy
time scales of this problem

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:56.843
there's no other way to bridge that gap.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:22:59.933
It was more fun eating the pizza.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:14.080
Next up, I head to the open seas

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:17.900
and then into the heart of Seattle

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.710
to learn how to afford a house here.

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:24.470
We bought it for $350,000,
transform the place,

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:25.690
and then we put it back on.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:29.417
Sold it in four days for about $450,000.

